{
  "design": {
    "theme": "studio",
    "picture_style": "photo"
  },
  "flow": [
    {
      "slide": 1,
      "does": "Recall encoding, storage and retrieval",
      "look_at": {"kind": "none", "shows": null},
      "teaches": []
    },
    {
      "slide": 2,
      "does": "Introduce the approved objectives",
      "look_at": {"kind": "none", "shows": null},
      "teaches": []
    },
    {
      "slide": 3,
      "does": "Teach the sensory register",
      "look_at": {"kind": "diagram", "shows": "Separate visual and auditory sensory traces"},
      "teaches": [1]
    },
    {
      "slide": 4,
      "does": "Compare short-term and long-term memory",
      "look_at": {"kind": "diagram", "shows": "Coding, capacity and duration across two stores"},
      "teaches": [1]
    },
    {
      "slide": 5,
      "does": "Check store properties through scenarios",
      "look_at": {"kind": "none", "shows": null},
      "teaches": [1]
    },
    {
      "slide": 6,
      "does": "Explain transfers between stores",
      "look_at": {"kind": "diagram", "shows": "Attention, rehearsal and retrieval pathways"},
      "teaches": [2]
    },
    {
      "slide": 7,
      "does": "Correct the idea that retrieval empties long-term memory",
      "look_at": {"kind": "diagram", "shows": "Recall makes a stored memory available in short-term memory"},
      "teaches": [2]
    },
    {
      "slide": 8,
      "does": "Introduce serial position evidence and predict a delay effect",
      "look_at": {"kind": "diagram", "shows": "The qualitative immediate-recall serial position curve"},
      "teaches": [3]
    },
    {
      "slide": 9,
      "does": "Model an evidence-based evaluation",
      "look_at": {"kind": "diagram", "shows": "Immediate and delayed recall patterns"},
      "teaches": [3]
    },
    {
      "slide": 10,
      "does": "Practise a closely matched evidence-based evaluation",
      "look_at": {"kind": "none", "shows": null},
      "teaches": [2, 3]
    },
    {
      "slide": 11,
      "does": "Teach KF as a challenge to one short-term store",
      "look_at": {"kind": "diagram", "shows": "Contrasting verbal and visual short-term memory outcomes"},
      "teaches": [3]
    },
    {
      "slide": 12,
      "does": "Apply the model and weigh evidence in a new case",
      "look_at": {"kind": "none", "shows": null},
      "teaches": [1, 2, 3]
    }
  ],
  "title": {
    "template": "title",
    "heading": "The multi-store model of memory",
    "lead": "Recall: what do encoding, storage and retrieval mean?",
    "picture": null
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "The sensory register",
      "lead": "Atkinson and Shiffrin (1968) proposed separate memory stores.",
      "points": [
        {"label": "Coding", "text": "Modality-specific: visual or auditory, for example."},
        {"label": "Capacity and duration", "text": "Very large capacity; traces last briefly, depending on the sense."}
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "flow",
        "shows": "Visual and auditory input leave brief traces in different sensory registers.",
        "alt": "Visual input enters iconic memory; auditory input enters echoic memory.",
        "title": null,
        "nodes": ["Visual input", "Iconic: under 0.5 s", "Auditory input", "Echoic: about 2–4 s"],
        "links": [
          {"from": 0, "to": 1, "label": null},
          {"from": 2, "to": 3, "label": null}
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "big-visual",
      "heading": "Two stores, different properties",
      "lead": "Compare coding, capacity and duration.",
      "figure": {
        "kind": "table",
        "shows": "The typical properties of short-term and long-term memory in the multi-store model.",
        "alt": "Short-term memory is mainly acoustic, limited and brief; long-term memory is mainly semantic, potentially unlimited and lasting.",
        "title": null,
        "header": ["Property", "Short-term memory", "Long-term memory"],
        "rows": [
          ["Coding", "Mainly acoustic", "Mainly semantic"],
          ["Capacity", "About 7 ± 2 items", "Potentially unlimited"],
          ["Duration", "About 18–30 seconds", "Potentially a lifetime"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "question-set",
      "heading": "Identify the store",
      "questions": [
        "A visual trace disappears within half a second. Name the store and its coding.",
        "A person briefly holds seven spoken digits. Name the store and its usual duration without rehearsal.",
        "A person remembers a word’s meaning years later. Name the store and its usual coding and capacity."
      ],
      "instruction": "Answer using the model’s typical properties.",
      "picture": null
    },
    {
      "template": "big-visual",
      "heading": "How information moves",
      "lead": "Trace each route and explain what makes it happen.",
      "figure": {
        "kind": "flow",
        "shows": "The multi-store model with attention, maintenance rehearsal and retrieval.",
        "alt": "Attention transfers sensory input to short-term memory. Rehearsal keeps it active and supports long-term storage. Retrieval returns information to short-term memory.",
        "title": null,
        "nodes": ["Sensory register", "Short-term memory", "Long-term memory"],
        "links": [
          {"from": 0, "to": 1, "label": "Attention"},
          {"from": 1, "to": 1, "label": "Maintenance rehearsal"},
          {"from": 1, "to": 2, "label": "Prolonged rehearsal"},
          {"from": 2, "to": 1, "label": "Retrieval"},
          {"from": 0, "to": "out", "label": "Unattended"},
          {"from": 1, "to": "out", "label": "Forgetting"}
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "Retrieval is not removal",
      "lead": "Recalling your address does not erase it from long-term memory.",
      "points": [
        {"label": "Retrieval", "text": "Stored information becomes available in short-term memory."},
        {"label": "Explain", "text": "Why can you recall the same address again tomorrow?"}
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "flow",
        "shows": "An address remains stored in long-term memory while retrieval makes it available in short-term memory.",
        "alt": "An address stored in long-term memory is retrieved into short-term memory; it remains stored for later recall.",
        "title": null,
        "nodes": ["LTM: stored address", "STM: address in use"],
        "links": [
          {"from": 0, "to": 1, "label": "Retrieval"}
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "big-visual",
      "heading": "Which words are recalled?",
      "lead": "Predict how 30 seconds of counting before recall might change this pattern.",
      "figure": {
        "kind": "line-graph",
        "shows": "A schematic immediate-recall serial position curve, not original numerical data.",
        "alt": "Immediate recall is higher for words at the beginning and end of a list than for words in the middle.",
        "title": null,
        "x": {"label": "Position in word list", "min": 1, "max": 15, "step": 1},
        "y": {"label": "Recall", "min": 0, "max": 100, "step": 20},
        "y2": null,
        "qualitative": true,
        "series": [
          {
            "label": "Immediate recall",
            "points": [[1, 85], [3, 65], [5, 40], [8, 30], [11, 35], [13, 60], [15, 90]],
            "style": "line",
            "axis": "left"
          }
        ],
        "segments": null,
        "annotations": null,
        "intervals": null
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "Worked evaluation: serial position",
      "lead": "Glanzer and Cunitz (1966): a filled delay reduced the recency effect.",
      "points": [
        "Early words: more rehearsal → LTM → primacy.",
        "Final words: still in STM → recency.",
        "Different delay effects support separate stores."
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "line-graph",
        "shows": "Schematic immediate and 30-second filled-delay recall curves; these are not original numerical data.",
        "alt": "Both curves show good recall of early words. Immediate recall also shows good recall of final words, but delayed recall does not.",
        "title": null,
        "x": {"label": "Position in word list", "min": 1, "max": 15, "step": 1},
        "y": {"label": "Recall", "min": 0, "max": 100, "step": 20},
        "y2": null,
        "qualitative": true,
        "series": [
          {
            "label": "Immediate",
            "points": [[1, 85], [3, 65], [5, 40], [8, 30], [11, 35], [13, 60], [15, 90]],
            "style": "line",
            "axis": "left"
          },
          {
            "label": "30 s filled delay",
            "points": [[1, 80], [3, 60], [5, 35], [8, 25], [11, 25], [13, 25], [15, 25]],
            "style": "line",
            "axis": "left"
          }
        ],
        "segments": null,
        "annotations": null,
        "intervals": null
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "practice",
      "heading": "Your turn: evaluate the evidence",
      "questions": [
        "Glanzer and Cunitz found better immediate recall of early and final words than middle words. After 30 seconds of counting, the final-word advantage largely disappeared. Explain why.",
        "Write a short evaluation using this finding: state the evidence, explain it using the model, and link it to separate stores.",
        "Stretch: does this finding establish that maintenance rehearsal is the only route into LTM? Explain."
      ],
      "instruction": "Write independently. Use evidence → explanation → judgement.",
      "picture": null
    },
    {
      "template": "visual-text",
      "heading": "KF: is STM one store?",
      "lead": "Shallice and Warrington (1970) studied KF after brain injury.",
      "points": [
        "His verbal STM was severely impaired; visual STM was relatively preserved.",
        "This challenges a single, uniform STM store.",
        "One unusual case may not represent everyone."
      ],
      "figure": {
        "kind": "table",
        "shows": "The contrasting short-term memory outcomes reported for KF.",
        "alt": "KF had severely impaired verbal short-term memory but relatively preserved visual short-term memory.",
        "title": null,
        "header": ["Task type", "KF’s performance"],
        "rows": [
          ["Verbal", "Impaired"],
          ["Visual", "Preserved"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "template": "practice",
      "heading": "Apply and judge",
      "questions": [
        "A station announcement briefly leaves an auditory trace. Maya attends to a platform number and repeats it silently. Explain the stores and processes involved.",
        "Maya later recalls the platform number without hearing it again. Explain this using the model, including typical LTM coding and duration.",
        "A new patient has poor memory for spoken digits but much better memory for visual patterns. Use KF and the serial position findings to judge whether the whole model should be rejected."
      ],
      "instruction": "Explain each answer, then complete the worksheet exit ticket.",
      "picture": null
    }
  ]
}
